# Line endings are LF in the WORKING TREE on every platform -- not merely in the
# index, which is what the default `text=auto` alone guarantees.
#
# This is a correctness requirement, not a style preference. patches/ is applied
# onto the chromium checkout with `git apply`, and apply_patches.py decides a
# patch is ALREADY applied by running `git apply --reverse --check` first, which
# needs the patch's context lines to match the checked-out file byte for byte. A
# Windows clone made under the Git-for-Windows default (core.autocrlf=true)
# materializes all ~120 patch files as CRLF while the chromium checkout stays
# LF, so every patch both fails to apply AND fails to reverse-apply -- and
# apply_patches.py's fail-fast turns that into "patch does not apply cleanly" on
# a tree that is in fact perfectly fine. Worse, `git status` reports the repo
# clean throughout, because the index is still LF: nothing in the repo shows
# that anything is wrong.
#
# Pinning eol=lf here makes that independent of each developer's core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: excluded from any conversion or diffing. text=auto would
# auto-detect these correctly, but stating it means a new asset type cannot be
# corrupted by a heuristic that guessed wrong.
*.png  binary
*.icns binary
*.tiff binary
